 6. 💊 Big midterm target: Health care companies 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The willingness to confront powerful health care interests is becoming a litmus test for candidates in congressional battleground races, Axios' Adriel Bettelheim and Caitlin Owens write. 

 Why it matters: Anxiety over health care costs is driving a new brand of populism as Republicans and Democrats try to outdo each other with tough messages about taking on drug companies, insurers and pharmacy benefit managers. Among the big races focused on cost and coverage:

 🧤 1. Michigan: This is easily the nation's largest bellwether for the popularity — or toxicity — of Medicare for All.

 Democrat Abdul El-Sayed,  a former public health official, is backing "Medicare for All" and steps like canceling medical debt, cracking down on hospital mergers and expanding Medicare drug price negotiations. His opponent, Republican Mike Rogers, is calling for penalties on health insurers found to have excessive denials or delays. 🌽 2. Iowa: Both candidates have embraced populist anti-industry messaging.

 In her first ad of the campaign, Republican Ashley Hinson declared that "health care companies are ripping you off." Democrat Josh Turek, who was born with spina bifida, has made affordability the defining theme of the campaign and has vowed to "take on" Big Pharma and pharmacy benefit managers. 🗳️ Go deeper : 6 more races to watch. 

 
 
